## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical online data-structure thinking for a sliding-window distinct problem, common in real-time analytics pipelines used by data platforms at Snowflake, Salesforce, and Instacart.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance
- Hash map with counts
- Eviction policy and amortized analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
let window = []; // deque of last W ids
let counts = new Map();
function add(id){
  window.push(id);
  counts.set(id, (counts.get(id) || 0) + 1);
  if (window.length > W){
    const old = window.shift();
    const c = counts.get(old) - 1;
    if (c === 0) counts.delete(old); else counts.set(old,c);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for approximate distinct in extremely high-throughput streams?
- What happens if events arrive in irregular batch sizes or with possible duplicates within a batch?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming heavy hitters and fixed-memory reasoning. Misra-Gries demonstrates how to bound error while tracking potential mode candidates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries algorithm; heavy-hitters with fixed counters
- Online updates, O(1) amortized per element
- Memory O(k); approximate answer with additive error

## Code Example
```javascript
class MG {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){
    if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1);
    else if(this.map.size < this.k-1) this.map.set(x,1);
    else{ for(const [v,c] of Array.from(this.map)) this.map.set(v,c-1); for(const [v,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(v)<=0) this.map.delete(v);} }
  }
}
```

